 changing and all  these things it was like dunking my head  in cold water and i woke up very very  quickly to this reality that this boy  could be taken away from me he could die  and i was racked with fear from that and  i started to try and figure out why am I  here as a parent if I can't control his  environment if I can't keep him safe and  if you extrapolate that further and say  I can't control who he becomes why am I  here and I started to realize I think as  a parent you're just you're constantly  trying to define who your child is you  try not to project who you want them to  be don't put your point of view on them  you just want to try and define who they  are for themselves and as they grow you  redefine that and that became the really  the basis for Mike Shannon's character  through the whole film he has no clue of  what his son's doing or where he's going  he's just trying to figure it out and  have faith in the idea that he's going  someplace he should how do you balance  those incredibly strong emotions with  the genre in which you're working and  also the idea that and you've said this  before that you don't want to be a slave  to plot in fact you've said I think plot  is overrated so how do you go about  constructing a movie that's going to be  that's going to serve those emotions  that you feel very strongly about work  in the style in which you like to write  or make movies and also fit in the  conventions of what some people might  expect this movie to do or become right  the more I do this the more complex that  gets you know but because you can break  it into a few different things you can  break it into you know subverting genre  and you can you can talk about narrative  exposition and my desire to remove it at  all costs but I think the key thing to  talk about is just character behavior  you know in a screenplay you have two  things you have lines of action and  to dialogue and that's it and in the  lines of action you say the character  walks in sits down picks up his water  you just explain what he does it makes  no sense to say he walks in sits down  picks up his water because his mother  beat him when he was a kid you that  would be stupid why would you include  that because no one's going to see that  so why would you ever include that in  the dialogue I think there's a myth that  dialogue is somehow outside of behavior  it's the exact same and so if you've  made a rule for yourself as a writer  which I have which is to treat dialogue  strictly as behavior then you have to  look at the the situations that the  characters are in in these scenes and  you have to try and be as honest as you  can we very rarely save for press tours  say exactly what we want we're very very  they're very few times where this  calculated and we've had these things  thought out we usually speak in subtext  I'm going to talk about your actors in a  minute but I want to talk about your  collaborators because you are a writer  director and you have written and  directed all of your movies but there's  also a team around you that is key and  that team includes your cinematographer  Adam stone your production designer Chad  Keith on this film your composure David  Wingo and your producer Sarah green and  Brian Kavanaugh Jones so even though you  are the writer-director can you talk  about how you work with this group and  why they are so essential to what you're  able to do yeah a quick speech on the  front of it it's not just false humility  here you know people throw out the word  I'll tour and everything else and yeah  it's flattering anyone that's actually  been involved in the making of a film  knows that it pretty much everybody in  this room it's an absolute lie you know  to say this is a jeff nichols film  that's why i've never taken a possessory  credit to be honest because it's it's  false it's fiction if you took David  wingo my composure out of the equation  my films would feel differently if you  took Adam stone out of the equation my  films would feel differently so when  people say well that's a jeff nichols  film what they're really talking about  is this group of people and I I'd I mean  that through and through and through you  know I've been really lucky to slowly be  able to be  together a team that I trust and that  know me for who I am oh it's just like  you're in this really amazing band and  and you can look at each other's  performances and just say we're all in  sync we're all in rhythm we're all on  the same page and your lead vocalist is  Michael Shannon pretty much right I  assume you wrote is actually a lead  vocalist okay you write for him you  write with him how do you collaborate  with him and how did you find Jordan  labor her us your young boy you know  mike is Mike's one of the greatest  actors in the world period and I've  worked with some amazing actors you know  I worked with Matthew McConaughey i  worked with Jessica Chastain and and  Mike and I have a very unique  relationship we we don't talk very much  we don't really try and go into  backstory or anything else beyond the  necessities to pull off a scene we just  identify with one another and I think we  identify with with these types of  characters that I'm writing and I think  he makes me a better writer I think if  I've stated that there's you know a  narrative experiment that I'm attempting  with these films of trying to remove  exposition anything unnecessary you can  partly do that because you know Mike  Shannon is going to be there to catch  you if you fall in between the lines  Mike Shannon is going to be emitting  this information from his pores and and  that's a that's a pretty big safety net  as a filmmaker for Jaden you know I  typically recoil when I hear the term  child actor you know I was very lucky in  this film mud that I made to find these  two boys that felt like they almost just  walked out of the woods in fact one did  actually walk out of the woods but um  and it was my natural instinct to start  that casting process again you know we  saw the thousand kids throughout the  southwest and southeast and I met a ton  of them and I met some really great kids  and I met some kids that could could  even handle the lines here and there  you know and this was this was a  dangerous age though because this eight  there's a big difference between 8 and  13 and obviously now that you have seen  the film there's a point when when this  boy is supposed to become aware of his  place in the universe that's a very hard  thing to act that's a very hard thing to  pretend and when I met Jaden who came to  me through CAA I had like a 10 minute  meeting with him in Austin he came to  see me and I was immediately struck by  how aware he was of the situation we  were in he's obviously one of these  hyper intelligent kids but it wasn't  that he was performing he wasn't tap  dancing for me he just knew where he was  at and what what the what the score was  and you could see it in his eyes and I  knew that if he had that capacity that I  could mute him in the first half of film  stick his nose in a comic book not given  many lines and then when I turned him on  he'd be there for me and and I I think  it works let me ask you this last  question the name of the movie is  probably inspired by the 1930s or  earlier song leadbelly covered it  Creedence covered it I think Van  Morrison covered it is this a song you  heard growing up that stuck with you is  that the lyric that made you want to  name your movie after it yeah to be  honest it's not it's not as intelligent  an answer as it should be I just thought  was badass you know I I grew up listen  to this song grip listen to credence and  you know I actually had to go back and  discover the Leadbelly version but  that's when I was going through an alan  lomax period anyway but like you know  like i said i had this first image these  two guys in a car driving fast down a  rural southern road at night and very  quickly after that i thought i could  call it Midnight Special it just seemed  to evoke the tone in the mood that I was  going to go after you know the colors  and also just the feeling of a late  night drive-in movie you know that felt  fun to me that felt like something I  wanted to spend a few years of my life  working on it was a good couple hours to  watch jeff nichols thank you  thank you all for eyes for them now we  know
 

